1
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:26,700
going on here at Rykerr Medical. We'll start with a review of how we got here. We'll explain

2
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:32,240
where things are at and how this podcast fits into the picture. We'll paint a vision of what

3
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:37,600
we hope this project to become, and then we'll end it with a discussion about how you can get involved.

4
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:44,400
Rykerr Medical started with a book on ventilator management for pre-hospital folks.

5
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,700
We self-published that resource to help clinicians in the field gain a basic understanding of

6
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:54,980
ventilators and transport. The idea from the beginning was to make that a free resource for

7
00:01:54,980 --> 00:02:00,960
everyone. So we got set up as an LLC and started the webpage to have a place to host it. You can

8
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:06,720
access and download that book for free. Just head over to RykerrMedical.com and navigate to the

9
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:13,540
section on event management. Once we had the website started, we slowly began to add other

10
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:18,900
bits of content there, various resources for clinicians in the field. We've got some drug

11
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:24,620
references, examples of protocols, cheat sheets, that sort of thing. There's lots more in the works,

12
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:29,760
but it takes time and we got distracted by another idea that brought us to where we are now.

13
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:34,420
The thought struck us that there are lots of people out there working in the field

14
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:39,200
that have insight to offer, but there's not a good way for those motivated individuals

15
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,100
to share what they know without a lot of upfront effort to make it happen.

16
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:47,100
Our vent book, for example, has been out for over a year now,

17
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:49,760
and the reach we've been able to achieve is pretty limited.

18
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:55,120
That seems unfortunate as we imagine there are a great many medics and nurses out there

19
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:57,400
who would be stoked to come across this type of thing.

20
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:03,200
Our solution for that is to try and use this platform, which is slowly gaining attention,

21
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:07,620
to allow others to publicize what they have to offer to the greater community.

22
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:13,400
Taking that idea one step further, we thought it would be pretty rad to make it happen via

23
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,140
a continuing education exchange.

24
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:20,660
We could build out a CE program to give hours back to people who contribute their work.

25
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:27,080
The idea is to foster some sense of community that both provides valuable content and gives

26
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:28,820
a benefit back to those who do the work.

27
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:31,160
And of course, we want the thing to be free.

28
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,360
Now, if you follow us on social media, you may already know that we did get granted organizational

29
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:39,980
accreditation for a CE program through CAPSE.

30
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:44,620
That's the Commission on Accreditation for Pre-Hospital Continuing Education.

31
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:55,400
Unfortunately, and for a variety of reasons that we don't need to get into, CAPSE has revoked that accreditation until we add a few more pieces to the puzzle and resubmit our application.

32
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:57,600
So that's where we're at for now.

33
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:01,920
This podcast will eventually be part of that CE program.

34
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:05,500
Listeners will be able to pitch ideas to us for episodes.

35
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:11,140
We will work with them to make it happen, and then CE credit will be awarded in the exchange.

36
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:16,540
In addition, there'll be an option for the community at large to get credit for listening

37
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:21,820
to certain episodes and taking quizzes or some sort of competency assessment that accompany

38
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:22,480
those episodes.

39
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:27,420
For now, we don't have the capacity to award credit, but we are still looking for content

40
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:32,620
contributors to work with us through the process so that we can demonstrate to CAPSEE exactly

41
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:36,140
how the quality control aspects work on our end.

42
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:40,520
So if you're interested in that sort of thing, here's how it would happen.

43
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:49,560
Head on over to RykerrMedical.com, follow the links to the Con Ed program development page, and submit an activity application.

44
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:55,200
We will review that application and then get back to you on next steps for making it happen.

45
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:03,160
In the meantime, we here at Rykerr Medical have a few ideas for episodes and will try to contribute regularly to the show.

46
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:09,220
Now, it is worth noting that not all of the stuff we host on the show or on the website fall into the CE program.

47
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:17,300
We may choose to share things that are non-clinical or otherwise non-testable and therefore can't really be worked into the continuing education side of things.

48
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:19,280
So this show will be a mix of both.

49
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:26,340
That said, if you have an idea for an episode that probably won't meet criteria for the CE program, we're also game for that as well.

50
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:30,800
You can use the very same application to get in touch, and we'll take it from there.

51
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:37,460
As far as what sorts of things fall into the CE model, that takes a bit of explaining.

52
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:41,500
Our initial pitch to CAPSEA included seven activities.

53
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:45,400
First are the podcast and blog, which are pretty self-explanatory.

54
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:49,320
We'd give credit to folks for contributing episodes or posts.

55
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:54,780
We also envisioned a mentorship activity in which we could give credit to an experienced clinician

56
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:59,480
for building out a mentorship program for someone new to the field or with less experience.

57
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:02,500
Then there was the idea of annotated illustrations,

58
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:06,580
offering credit for explaining concepts visually or graphically.

59
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:16,840
We also had a reflection activity in which someone could share an experience via the platform in an active reflection or debrief, whether that be text, audio, or video.

60
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:26,660
Another idea was the critique, essentially a way for someone to informally peer review content produced by someone else and to provide constructive feedback on whatever it might be.

61
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:33,500
And lastly was the instructional design activity in which we give credit to people for building out courses or lessons.

62
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:38,760
Now the hope moving forward is to find a way to make all of these activities work.

63
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:43,980
The catch is that we now also have to have a mechanism to offer the community at large

64
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:49,560
the opportunity to earn credit for participating in or consuming whatever content we put out

65
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:50,800
through the CE program.

66
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:56,540
And just to be clear, this is at the request of CAPSE as the accrediting body, so there's

67
00:06:56,540 --> 00:06:57,940
no real workaround for us there.

68
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:08,040
The plan is to get content up on a learning management system or LMS so that anyone can access those final projects and participate in them for credit.

69
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:14,580
Which is cool and it means that we'll eventually have a library of stuff to meet whatever CE needs you might have.

70
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:22,680
The downside or the problem to be overcome is that not all of these activities we initially envisioned are readily testable.

71
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:27,820
So let's say you want to do a reflection on a run you had and how it affected you personally.

72
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:31,880
That's not something that lends itself to educational goals or learning outcomes

73
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:35,580
that we could put down on a lesson plan for others to get credit from,

74
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:38,940
which is totally okay and we're still down to host your story.

75
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:43,560
It just means that we've got to sort out which contributions will be part of the CE program

76
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:48,660
and which will be just for the sake of getting it out there into the universe and sharing the experience.

77
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:53,060
Another problematic example is the mentorship program.

78
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:58,120
It's a bit tough to test outsiders on a one-on-one mentorship session.

79
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:03,720
That said, we can work to design those plans to focus on specific goals or objectives.

80
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:09,880
We could then record those sessions and share via the LMS like any other asynchronous course.

81
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:16,040
In fact, there's also the option to live stream them so that participants can get credit in real time.

82
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:21,600
In that scenario, we'd be able to give credit to the mentor for developing and guiding the session,

83
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:30,160
to the live audience for participating synchronously, and then to the stragglers who watch it asynchronously via the LMS.

84
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:36,120
We've been toying with this idea along with some of our partners, so more on that whenever we get it worked out.

85
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:42,320
But to summarize where things are at currently, we're going to make this CE program happen one way or another,

86
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:44,360
and when we do, it'll be badass.

87
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,600
In the meantime, we'll work on getting this podcast and the blog going and are open to any help we can get.

88
00:08:50,600 --> 00:09:00,500
The end goal or vision for it all is that we will exist as a home for high quality learning opportunities created for and by a community of clinicians.

89
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:10,900
We can help hash out ideas and refine projects to meet industry standards and requirements of accreditation, but our hope is to eventually become a host and let you guys keep the things going.

90
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:16,720
We're here to get it all started, to do the logistics and maintain accreditation, and

91
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:19,640
then we'll probably still want to pitch in every now and again with new thoughts and

92
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:20,780
ideas as they come up.

93
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:26,840
Basically, we want to break the mold that keeps ground-level providers from pitching

94
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:28,340
in on the FOMED community.

95
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:33,480
Lots of folks have stuff to offer, but not everyone has an in to make it happen.

96
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:38,600
And then oftentimes it's the case that someone has an idea in the works, but not a good way

97
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:40,820
to collaborate on and develop that idea.

98
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:43,980
That for sure was the case when we put the vent book together.

99
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:48,220
There wasn't an established mechanism to work with peers on those ideas.

100
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:52,440
Hopefully, Rykerr Medical can be that supportive community for others.

101
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:00,140
We want to both give clinicians a way to contribute and support them in that effort via constructive and cooperative approach.

102
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:07,020
And then to take that concept just a bit further, we're hoping to make the CE program as affordable as possible.

103
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:11,580
The content development side of the house will for sure be put out for free,

104
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:15,940
and all the things we create will be openly available to anyone with a computer.

105
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:20,300
We're going to try and make the CEs that go with those things free also,

106
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:22,740
but that's dependent on a handful of factors.

107
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:28,020
So it might end up just being ridiculously cheap until we can get funding worked out.

108
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:31,360
And then there's one other part of the vision.

109
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:36,280
CAPSE is the accrediting body for EMS stuff, and that's where we're starting.

110
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:39,940
but there are also avenues to get CEs for our nursing colleagues,

111
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:43,660
and there are specialty boards that many of us use for maintenance of certifications.

112
00:10:44,420 --> 00:10:47,220
We'll get there eventually, but for now it's one step at a time.

113
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:50,400
So that's the general idea.

114
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:53,940
Now you know how we got here, where things are at with us,

115
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:56,300
and what we're hoping to make happen.

116
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:58,760
Next step is how you can get involved.

117
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:01,180
First and foremost, check out the website.

118
00:11:01,660 --> 00:11:04,820
You can always get in touch with us there, or on social media,

119
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:08,380
or by adding comments here, wherever you happen to be listening to this.

120
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:11,840
We are open to any and all ideas or suggestions.

121
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:16,140
But more specifically, we need to get some content up

122
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:18,300
in order to resubmit our application to CAPSE.

123
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:21,240
We've got details about that on the website,

124
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:25,920
but we need examples of each of the activities we want to include in the program.

125
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:27,460
And the more, the merrier.

126
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:32,200
So if any of them sound appealing to you, hit us up and we can make it happen.

127
00:11:32,860 --> 00:11:38,680
We also need help figuring out exactly how to host all the things and track users in the system.

128
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:45,620
So if you have experience working with the LMS side of things and want to share what you know, we could use some help there too.

129
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:50,640
And then lastly, we also need people to lend a hand as content reviewers.

130
00:11:51,100 --> 00:12:00,160
As activities get rolling, we have a cadre of volunteers that maintain a dialogue with participants to offer feedback and guidance as the projects start to take form.

131
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:06,180
And then once submitted, we review them internally to make sure everything is copacetic and meets

132
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:07,500
all the standards we are held to.

133
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:12,300
If that sounds like something you're into, just let us know and we can set up a time

134
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:12,940
to discuss.

135
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:15,900
And that's pretty much it.

136
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:17,360
Thanks for tuning in.

137
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:20,740
But real quick before we go, just a few shout outs.

138
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:25,560
First is to Pragmatic Paramedics, another awesome podcast that we highly recommend.

139
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:29,480
Next is to the St. Fisher Church of Evidence-Based Medicine.

140
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:32,460
If you don't follow them already, definitely check them out.

141
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:38,140
These guys, both Pragmatic Paramedics and the St. Fisher folks, have been instrumental

142
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:41,180
in getting our stuff out there into the world, and we truly appreciate that.

143
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:46,060
And then we'll end things with a shout out to my good friend Josh Pittman and his buddy

144
00:12:46,060 --> 00:12:46,740
Kevin Pons.

145
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:48,900
They put the music together for the show.

146
00:12:49,420 --> 00:12:50,180
Until next time.

147
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:29,460
Thank you.
